(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("go",{"go/why-go-and-your-first-program":{"html":"\u003cp>Docker, Kubernetes, Terraform, Prometheus and many other tools you use every day are written in Go. When you read their code, write an operator, or build a small internal tool that must run on any server without installing a runtime, Go is the natural choice. It is a small language with fast builds, built-in concurrency and one standard way to format, test and build code, which makes it easy to pick up for someone who already scripts in Bash or Python.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why Go suits operations tools\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Create a module and run, build and cross-compile a program\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use gofmt, go vet and go mod as part of the normal workflow\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Read a small Go program: packages, imports, main\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-operations-teams-like-go\">Why operations teams like Go\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Go compiles to a \u003cstrong>single static binary\u003c/strong> with no interpreter or shared libraries to install, so you can copy one file to a server or put it into an almost empty container image. Compilation is fast, the standard library already includes an HTTP server and client, JSON, testing, and crypto, and goroutines make it practical to handle many connections or checks at once. The language is deliberately small, so code written by different people looks alike. The price is verbosity (explicit error handling) and a less expressive type system than some languages. For glue scripts of ten lines, Bash or Python is still faster to write; Go pays off for tools that others will run, long-running services and anything that must be fast and dependable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-module-and-a-first-program\">A module and a first program\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A Go project is a \u003cstrong>module\u003c/strong>, described by a \u003ccode>go.mod\u003c/code> file. Code lives in packages, and an executable has a \u003ccode>main\u003c/code> package with a \u003ccode>main\u003c/code> function.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"mkdir diskcheck &amp;&amp; cd diskcheck\ngo mod init example.com/ops/diskcheck\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">mkdir\u003c/span> diskcheck &amp;&amp; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cd\u003c/span> diskcheck\ngo mod init example.com/ops/diskcheck\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"package main\n\nimport (\n    &quot;fmt&quot;\n    &quot;os&quot;\n)\n\nfunc main() {\n    host, err := os.Hostname()\n    if err != nil {\n        fmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, &quot;cannot read hostname:&quot;, err)\n        os.Exit(1)\n    }\n    fmt.Printf(&quot;checking %s\\n&quot;, host)\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">package\u003c/span> main\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> (\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;fmt&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;os&quot;\u003c/span>\n)\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title\">main\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">()\u003c/span>\u003c/span> {\n    host, err := os.Hostname()\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> err != \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">nil\u003c/span> {\n        fmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cannot read hostname:&quot;\u003c/span>, err)\n        os.Exit(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>)\n    }\n    fmt.Printf(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;checking %s\\n&quot;\u003c/span>, host)\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"go run .                 # compile and run\ngo build -o diskcheck .  # produce a binary\n./diskcheck\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">go run .                 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># compile and run\u003c/span>\ngo build -o diskcheck .  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># produce a binary\u003c/span>\n./diskcheck\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Notice the pattern: a function returns a value and an \u003ccode>error\u003c/code>, and the caller checks it right away. You will see it everywhere in Go.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"build-for-any-platform\">Build for any platform\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cross-compiling needs two environment variables and no extra tools:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"GOOS=linux GOARCH=amd64 go build -o diskcheck-linux-amd64 .\nGOOS=linux GOARCH=arm64 go build -o diskcheck-linux-arm64 .\nGOOS=darwin GOARCH=arm64 go build -o diskcheck-mac .\nfile diskcheck-linux-amd64\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">GOOS=linux GOARCH=amd64 go build -o diskcheck-linux-amd64 .\nGOOS=linux GOARCH=arm64 go build -o diskcheck-linux-arm64 .\nGOOS=darwin GOARCH=arm64 go build -o diskcheck-mac .\nfile diskcheck-linux-amd64\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>From a laptop you can produce binaries for the servers you manage. Set \u003ccode>CGO_ENABLED=0\u003c/code> when you want a fully static binary that does not depend on the C library, for example for a minimal container image. Check the result with \u003ccode>ldd\u003c/code>, which should say &quot;not a dynamic executable&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-standard-toolbox\">The standard toolbox\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Go ships with its tools, so teams rarely argue about style or setup:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>gofmt -l .\u003c/code> lists files that are not formatted; \u003ccode>gofmt -w .\u003c/code> fixes them. Most editors do it on save.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>go vet ./...\u003c/code> finds suspicious code, such as a wrong format string.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>go test ./...\u003c/code> runs tests in every package.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>go mod tidy\u003c/code> adds missing and removes unused dependencies in \u003ccode>go.mod\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>go doc fmt.Printf\u003c/code> shows documentation in the terminal.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Dependencies are fetched by version from module proxies and recorded with checksums in \u003ccode>go.sum\u003c/code>, so builds are reproducible. Commit both \u003ccode>go.mod\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>go.sum\u003c/code>. You can practise small exercises in the browser on the Go playground page of this site before installing anything.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You need to give a teammate a tool that runs on their Linux servers without installing anything. Why is Go a good fit?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> It builds a single static binary, so you copy one file and run it, with no runtime to install.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Static, self-contained binaries are the main operational advantage.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-tool-that-needed-python-3-11\">Incident: The tool that needed Python 3.11\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An internal inventory script worked on laptops and failed on half the servers.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"./inventory.py\npython3 --version\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>./inventory.py\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Traceback (most recent call last):\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  File &quot;./inventory.py&quot;, line 3, in &lt;module&gt;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    import tomllib\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'tomllib'\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python3 --version\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Python 3.8.10\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The script needed a newer interpreter than the older servers had. The team rewrote the tool in Go, built one binary per architecture with GOOS and GOARCH, and shipped it through the artifact store. Nothing needs to be installed on the servers, and the same binary is used in CI and in the container image.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Forgetting to run go mod init so imports fail\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring the error return value\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Building on a laptop with CGO enabled and then running on a different C library\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not committing go.sum\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"why-operations-teams-like-go","text":"Why operations teams like Go"},{"id":"a-module-and-a-first-program","text":"A module and a first program"},{"id":"build-for-any-platform","text":"Build for any platform"},{"id":"the-standard-toolbox","text":"The standard toolbox"},{"id":"incident-the-tool-that-needed-python-3-11","text":"Incident: The tool that needed Python 3.11"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"A Tour of Go","u":"https://go.dev/tour/"},{"t":"Tutorial: Get started with Go","u":"https://go.dev/doc/tutorial/getting-started"}],"text":"Docker, Kubernetes, Terraform, Prometheus and many other tools you use every day are written in Go. When you read their code, write an operator, or build a small internal tool that must run on any server without installing a runtime, Go is the natural choice. It is a small language with fast builds, built-in concurrency and one standard way to format, test and build code, which makes it easy to pick up for someone who already scripts in Bash or Python. What you will learn Why Go suits operations tools Create a module and run, build and cross-compile a program Use gofmt, go vet and go mod as part of the normal workflow Read a small Go program: packages, imports, main Why operations teams like Go Go compiles to a single static binary with no interpreter or shared libraries to install, so you can copy one file to a server or put it into an almost empty container image. Compilation is fast, the standard library already includes an HTTP server and client, JSON, testing, and crypto, and goroutines make it practical to handle many connections or checks at once. The language is deliberately small, so code written by different people looks alike. The price is verbosity (explicit error handling) and a less expressive type system than some languages. For glue scripts of ten lines, Bash or Python is still faster to write; Go pays off for tools that others will run, long-running services and anything that must be fast and dependable. A module and a first program A Go project is a module , described by a go.mod file. Code lives in packages, and an executable has a main package with a main function. mkdir diskcheck && cd diskcheck go mod init example.com/ops/diskcheck package main import ( \"fmt\" \"os\" ) func main () { host, err := os.Hostname() if err != nil { fmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, \"cannot read hostname:\" , err) os.Exit( 1 ) } fmt.Printf( \"checking %s\\n\" , host) } go run . # compile and run go build -o diskcheck . # produce a binary ./diskcheck Notice the pattern: a function returns a value and an error , and the caller checks it right away. You will see it everywhere in Go. Build for any platform Cross-compiling needs two environment variables and no extra tools: GOOS=linux GOARCH=amd64 go build -o diskcheck-linux-amd64 . GOOS=linux GOARCH=arm64 go build -o diskcheck-linux-arm64 . GOOS=darwin GOARCH=arm64 go build -o diskcheck-mac . file diskcheck-linux-amd64 From a laptop you can produce binaries for the servers you manage. Set CGO_ENABLED=0 when you want a fully static binary that does not depend on the C library, for example for a minimal container image. Check the result with ldd , which should say \"not a dynamic executable\". The standard toolbox Go ships with its tools, so teams rarely argue about style or setup: gofmt -l . lists files that are not formatted; gofmt -w . fixes them. Most editors do it on save. go vet ./... finds suspicious code, such as a wrong format string. go test ./... runs tests in every package. go mod tidy adds missing and removes unused dependencies in go.mod . go doc fmt.Printf shows documentation in the terminal. Dependencies are fetched by version from module proxies and recorded with checksums in go.sum , so builds are reproducible. Commit both go.mod and go.sum . You can practise small exercises in the browser on the Go playground page of this site before installing anything. Predict first. You need to give a teammate a tool that runs on their Linux servers without installing anything. Why is Go a good fit? Your guess Answer: It builds a single static binary, so you copy one file and run it, with no runtime to install. Static, self-contained binaries are the main operational advantage. Incident: The tool that needed Python 3.11 Incident An internal inventory script worked on laptops and failed on half the servers. $ ./inventory.py Traceback (most recent call last): File \"./inventory.py\", line 3, in \u003cmodule> import tomllib ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'tomllib' $ python3 --version Python 3.8.10 The "},"go/types-structs-slices-and-maps":{"html":"\u003cp>Most operations tools read data, shape it and print or send it: a list of hosts, a map of settings, a record for each disk. Go gives you a handful of simple building blocks for that. They have a few behaviours that surprise people coming from Python, such as zero values, slices that share memory, and maps that are not safe to use from several goroutines at once. Learn those and you avoid most early bugs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Variables, constants and zero values\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Slices: length, capacity, append and copying\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Maps: lookup with the comma-ok form\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Structs and methods, including pointer receivers\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"variables-and-zero-values\">Variables and zero values\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Declare with \u003ccode>var\u003c/code> or the short form \u003ccode>:=\u003c/code> inside functions. Every type has a \u003cstrong>zero value\u003c/strong> (0, &quot;&quot;, false, nil), so a variable is never uninitialized.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"var count int            // 0\nvar name string          // &quot;&quot;\nthreshold := 0.85        // float64, inferred\nconst maxRetries = 3\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">var\u003c/span> count \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">int\u003c/span>            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">// 0\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">var\u003c/span> name \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">string\u003c/span>          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">// &quot;&quot;\u003c/span>\nthreshold := \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.85\u003c/span>        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">// float64, inferred\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">const\u003c/span> maxRetries = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Types are strict: an \u003ccode>int\u003c/code> and an \u003ccode>int64\u003c/code> cannot be mixed without conversion (\u003ccode>int64(count)\u003c/code>). Strings are immutable sequences of bytes, usually UTF-8, and \u003ccode>len(s)\u003c/code> counts bytes, not characters. Use \u003ccode>strings\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>strconv\u003c/code> from the standard library for most text work, for example \u003ccode>strconv.Atoi(&quot;42&quot;)\u003c/code> which returns a number and an error.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"slices\">Slices\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A slice is a window onto an array, with a length and a capacity.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"hosts := []string{&quot;web1&quot;, &quot;web2&quot;}\nhosts = append(hosts, &quot;web3&quot;)     // append may allocate a new array\nfor i, h := range hosts {\n    fmt.Println(i, h)\n}\nfirstTwo := hosts[:2]             // shares memory with hosts\nsafe := append([]string(nil), hosts...) // an independent copy\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">hosts := []\u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">string\u003c/span>{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web1&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web2&quot;\u003c/span>}\nhosts = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">append\u003c/span>(hosts, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web3&quot;\u003c/span>)     \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">// append may allocate a new array\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> i, h := \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">range\u003c/span> hosts {\n    fmt.Println(i, h)\n}\nfirstTwo := hosts[:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>]             \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">// shares memory with hosts\u003c/span>\nsafe := \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">append\u003c/span>([]\u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">string\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">nil\u003c/span>), hosts...) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">// an independent copy\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Two gotchas. First, a sub-slice shares the underlying array, so changing \u003ccode>firstTwo[0]\u003c/code> changes \u003ccode>hosts[0]\u003c/code>; copy when you need independence. Second, always assign the result of \u003ccode>append\u003c/code>, because the slice header you passed may be out of date. A nil slice is valid and behaves like an empty one in \u003ccode>len\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>range\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>append\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"maps\">Maps\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A map is a hash table with unordered keys.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ports := map[string]int{&quot;http&quot;: 80, &quot;https&quot;: 443}\nports[&quot;ssh&quot;] = 22\n\nif p, ok := ports[&quot;smtp&quot;]; ok {\n    fmt.Println(&quot;smtp on&quot;, p)\n} else {\n    fmt.Println(&quot;smtp not configured&quot;)\n}\ndelete(ports, &quot;ssh&quot;)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ports := \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">map\u003c/span>[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">string\u003c/span>]\u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">int\u003c/span>{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;http&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">80\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;https&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">443\u003c/span>}\nports[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ssh&quot;\u003c/span>] = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">22\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> p, ok := ports[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;smtp&quot;\u003c/span>]; ok {\n    fmt.Println(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;smtp on&quot;\u003c/span>, p)\n} \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span> {\n    fmt.Println(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;smtp not configured&quot;\u003c/span>)\n}\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">delete\u003c/span>(ports, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ssh&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Reading a missing key returns the zero value, so use the \u003cstrong>comma-ok\u003c/strong> form when &quot;missing&quot; and &quot;zero&quot; mean different things. Writing to a nil map panics, so create one with \u003ccode>make(map[string]int)\u003c/code> or a literal. Iteration order is random on purpose; sort the keys (package \u003ccode>sort\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>slices\u003c/code>) when you need stable output. A plain map is not safe for concurrent writes, which matters in the lesson on goroutines.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"structs-and-methods\">Structs and methods\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A struct groups fields; methods attach behaviour.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"type Disk struct {\n    Mount   string\n    UsedPct float64\n}\n\nfunc (d Disk) Full() bool { return d.UsedPct &gt;= 90 }\n\nfunc (d *Disk) Grow(p float64) { d.UsedPct += p }\n\nd := Disk{Mount: &quot;/var&quot;, UsedPct: 87}\nd.Grow(5)\nfmt.Println(d.Full())     // true\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">type\u003c/span> Disk \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">struct\u003c/span> {\n    Mount   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">string\u003c/span>\n    UsedPct \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">float64\u003c/span>\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">(d Disk)\u003c/span>\u003c/span> Full() \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">bool\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> d.UsedPct &gt;= \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">90\u003c/span> }\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">(d *Disk)\u003c/span>\u003c/span> Grow(p \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">float64\u003c/span>) { d.UsedPct += p }\n\nd := Disk{Mount: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/var&quot;\u003c/span>, UsedPct: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">87\u003c/span>}\nd.Grow(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>)\nfmt.Println(d.Full())     \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">// true\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>value receiver\u003c/strong> works on a copy; a \u003cstrong>pointer receiver\u003c/strong> can change the original and avoids copying large structs. Mixing both on one type is confusing, so most types use pointer receivers if any method needs to modify state. Exported names start with a capital letter (\u003ccode>Mount\u003c/code>); lowercase names are private to the package. Struct tags such as \u003ccode>`json:&quot;mount&quot;`\u003c/code> control how encoding libraries name fields.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You slice `hosts[:2]` and change the first element of the slice. What happens to `hosts`?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> It changes too, because the sub-slice shares the same underlying array. Copy it if you need independence.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Slices are windows, not copies.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-map-that-panicked-at-startup\">Incident: The map that panicked at startup\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new service crashed on its first request with a panic.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">panic: assignment to entry in nil map\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">goroutine 18 [running]:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">main.(*Registry).Add(...)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    /src/registry.go:22\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">main.handle({0xc0001a2000, 0x1c})\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The Registry struct had a map field that was never created, because a struct&#39;s zero value leaves maps nil. Reading from a nil map is fine, so unit tests passed; the first write panicked. The fix was a constructor function that calls make, and a test that adds an entry. The team also added a lint rule for exported structs with map fields.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Writing to a nil map\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming a sub-slice is a copy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting to assign the result of append\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Relying on map iteration order\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"variables-and-zero-values","text":"Variables and zero values"},{"id":"slices","text":"Slices"},{"id":"maps","text":"Maps"},{"id":"structs-and-methods","text":"Structs and methods"},{"id":"incident-the-map-that-panicked-at-startup","text":"Incident: The map that panicked at startup"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Go Tour: Slices and Maps","u":"https://go.dev/tour/moretypes/1"},{"t":"Go blog: Go Slices, usage and internals","u":"https://go.dev/blog/slices-intro"}],"text":"Most operations tools read data, shape it and print or send it: a list of hosts, a map of settings, a record for each disk. Go gives you a handful of simple building blocks for that. They have a few behaviours that surprise people coming from Python, such as zero values, slices that share memory, and maps that are not safe to use from several goroutines at once. Learn those and you avoid most early bugs. What you will learn Variables, constants and zero values Slices: length, capacity, append and copying Maps: lookup with the comma-ok form Structs and methods, including pointer receivers Variables and zero values Declare with var or the short form := inside functions. Every type has a zero value (0, \"\", false, nil), so a variable is never uninitialized. var count int // 0 var name string // \"\" threshold := 0.85 // float64, inferred const maxRetries = 3 Types are strict: an int and an int64 cannot be mixed without conversion ( int64(count) ). Strings are immutable sequences of bytes, usually UTF-8, and len(s) counts bytes, not characters. Use strings and strconv from the standard library for most text work, for example strconv.Atoi(\"42\") which returns a number and an error. Slices A slice is a window onto an array, with a length and a capacity. hosts := [] string { \"web1\" , \"web2\" } hosts = append (hosts, \"web3\" ) // append may allocate a new array for i, h := range hosts { fmt.Println(i, h) } firstTwo := hosts[: 2 ] // shares memory with hosts safe := append ([] string ( nil ), hosts...) // an independent copy Two gotchas. First, a sub-slice shares the underlying array, so changing firstTwo[0] changes hosts[0] ; copy when you need independence. Second, always assign the result of append , because the slice header you passed may be out of date. A nil slice is valid and behaves like an empty one in len , range and append . Maps A map is a hash table with unordered keys. ports := map [ string ] int { \"http\" : 80 , \"https\" : 443 } ports[ \"ssh\" ] = 22 if p, ok := ports[ \"smtp\" ]; ok { fmt.Println( \"smtp on\" , p) } else { fmt.Println( \"smtp not configured\" ) } delete (ports, \"ssh\" ) Reading a missing key returns the zero value, so use the comma-ok form when \"missing\" and \"zero\" mean different things. Writing to a nil map panics, so create one with make(map[string]int) or a literal. Iteration order is random on purpose; sort the keys (package sort or slices ) when you need stable output. A plain map is not safe for concurrent writes, which matters in the lesson on goroutines. Structs and methods A struct groups fields; methods attach behaviour. type Disk struct { Mount string UsedPct float64 } func (d Disk) Full() bool { return d.UsedPct >= 90 } func (d *Disk) Grow(p float64 ) { d.UsedPct += p } d := Disk{Mount: \"/var\" , UsedPct: 87 } d.Grow( 5 ) fmt.Println(d.Full()) // true A value receiver works on a copy; a pointer receiver can change the original and avoids copying large structs. Mixing both on one type is confusing, so most types use pointer receivers if any method needs to modify state. Exported names start with a capital letter ( Mount ); lowercase names are private to the package. Struct tags such as `json:\"mount\"` control how encoding libraries name fields. Predict first. You slice `hosts[:2]` and change the first element of the slice. What happens to `hosts`? Your guess Answer: It changes too, because the sub-slice shares the same underlying array. Copy it if you need independence. Slices are windows, not copies. Incident: The map that panicked at startup Incident A new service crashed on its first request with a panic. panic: assignment to entry in nil map goroutine 18 [running]: main.(*Registry).Add(...) /src/registry.go:22 main.handle({0xc0001a2000, 0x1c}) The Registry struct had a map field that was never created, because a struct&#39;s zero value leaves maps nil. Reading from a nil map is fine, so unit tests passed; the first write panicked. The fix was a constructor function that calls make, and a test that adds an e"},"go/functions-errors-and-defer":{"html":"\u003cp>Go has no exceptions for ordinary failures. A function that can fail returns an error as its last value, and the caller decides what to do. This feels repetitive for a day and then becomes a strength: every failure path is visible in the code, which is exactly what you want in software that runs production. This lesson covers the habits that make that style readable: wrapping, inspecting, and cleaning up with defer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Functions with multiple return values\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Return, wrap and inspect errors with %w, errors.Is and errors.As\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use defer for cleanup and understand its order\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>When panic and recover are appropriate\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"errors-are-values\">Errors are values\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"func readConfig(path string) (Config, error) {\n    data, err := os.ReadFile(path)\n    if err != nil {\n        return Config{}, fmt.Errorf(&quot;read config %s: %w&quot;, path, err)\n    }\n    var cfg Config\n    if err := json.Unmarshal(data, &amp;cfg); err != nil {\n        return Config{}, fmt.Errorf(&quot;parse config %s: %w&quot;, path, err)\n    }\n    return cfg, nil\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title\">readConfig\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">(path \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">string\u003c/span>)\u003c/span>\u003c/span> (Config, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">error\u003c/span>) {\n    data, err := os.ReadFile(path)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> err != \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">nil\u003c/span> {\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> Config{}, fmt.Errorf(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;read config %s: %w&quot;\u003c/span>, path, err)\n    }\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">var\u003c/span> cfg Config\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> err := json.Unmarshal(data, &amp;cfg); err != \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">nil\u003c/span> {\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> Config{}, fmt.Errorf(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;parse config %s: %w&quot;\u003c/span>, path, err)\n    }\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> cfg, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">nil\u003c/span>\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Check \u003ccode>err != nil\u003c/code> right after the call and return early. Add context with \u003ccode>fmt.Errorf\u003c/code> so the final message tells a story: \u003ccode>read config /etc/app.json: open /etc/app.json: permission denied\u003c/code>. The verb \u003ccode>%w\u003c/code> wraps the original error so it can still be inspected. Never ignore an error with \u003ccode>_\u003c/code> unless you can explain why it cannot matter.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"inspect-errors\">Inspect errors\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"_, err := os.Open(&quot;/etc/app.json&quot;)\nif errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) {\n    // use defaults\n} else if err != nil {\n    return err\n}\n\nvar pe *fs.PathError\nif errors.As(err, &amp;pe) {\n    fmt.Println(&quot;failed path:&quot;, pe.Path)\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">_, err := os.Open(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/etc/app.json&quot;\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">// use defaults\u003c/span>\n} \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> err != \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">nil\u003c/span> {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> err\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">var\u003c/span> pe *fs.PathError\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> errors.As(err, &amp;pe) {\n    fmt.Println(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;failed path:&quot;\u003c/span>, pe.Path)\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>errors.Is\u003c/code> compares an error (and everything it wraps) with a known value. \u003ccode>errors.As\u003c/code> finds an error of a specific type in the chain. You can define your own sentinel error with \u003ccode>var ErrNoQuota = errors.New(&quot;no quota&quot;)\u003c/code> or your own type with an \u003ccode>Error() string\u003c/code> method. Do not compare error strings; they change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"defer\">Defer\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>defer\u003c/code> schedules a call to run when the surrounding function returns, whatever the path.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"func copyFile(src, dst string) error {\n    in, err := os.Open(src)\n    if err != nil {\n        return err\n    }\n    defer in.Close()\n\n    out, err := os.Create(dst)\n    if err != nil {\n        return err\n    }\n    defer out.Close()\n\n    _, err = io.Copy(out, in)\n    return err\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title\">copyFile\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">(src, dst \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">string\u003c/span>)\u003c/span>\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">error\u003c/span> {\n    in, err := os.Open(src)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> err != \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">nil\u003c/span> {\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> err\n    }\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">defer\u003c/span> in.Close()\n\n    out, err := os.Create(dst)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> err != \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">nil\u003c/span> {\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> err\n    }\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">defer\u003c/span> out.Close()\n\n    _, err = io.Copy(out, in)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> err\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Deferred calls run in last-in, first-out order. Arguments are evaluated when the defer statement runs, not when the call happens. Avoid defer inside a long loop, because the calls pile up until the function ends; move the body into a function instead. Deferred calls are how you release locks, close files, and stop timers without leaking them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"panic-and-recover\">Panic and recover\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>panic\u003c/code> stops normal flow and unwinds the stack. It is for programmer errors that should never happen (an impossible state, an index out of range) and not for &quot;file not found&quot;. A web server framework usually calls \u003ccode>recover\u003c/code> in middleware so that one bad request does not kill the process, and logs the stack trace. In your own code, prefer returning errors. At the top of \u003ccode>main\u003c/code>, print a clear message to standard error and exit with a non-zero code so scripts and orchestrators see the failure:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"if err := run(); err != nil {\n    fmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, &quot;error:&quot;, err)\n    os.Exit(1)\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> err := run(); err != \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">nil\u003c/span> {\n    fmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;error:&quot;\u003c/span>, err)\n    os.Exit(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>)\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A function returns `fmt.Errorf(&quot;open: %v&quot;, err)` instead of using %w. What do you lose?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The wrapped error. Callers can no longer use errors.Is or errors.As to detect the original cause.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use %w when the cause matters to callers.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-error-that-said-only-failed\">Incident: The error that said only &quot;failed&quot;\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A nightly job logged one word and no one could tell what failed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -n &quot;return err&quot; jobs/sync.go | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2026-01-05T02:00:07Z ERROR job failed\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;return err&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">jobs\u003c/span>/sync.go | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">41:\t\treturn err\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">58:\t\treturn err\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">77:\t\treturn errors.New(&quot;failed&quot;)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The code returned bare errors or a constant message, so the log had no hint about which step or file failed. The team adopted a rule: wrap every returned error with what the function was doing and the key identifier, and log once at the top. The same failure now reads &quot;sync users: fetch page 14: context deadline exceeded&quot;, which points directly at the slow call.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Ignoring errors with _\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Returning errors without context\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Comparing error text\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deferring in a loop and holding files open\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"errors-are-values","text":"Errors are values"},{"id":"inspect-errors","text":"Inspect errors"},{"id":"defer","text":"Defer"},{"id":"panic-and-recover","text":"Panic and recover"},{"id":"incident-the-error-that-said-only-failed","text":"Incident: The error that said only failed"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Go blog: Working with Errors in Go 1.13","u":"https://go.dev/blog/go1.13-errors"},{"t":"Effective Go: Errors and Defer","u":"https://go.dev/doc/effective_go#defer"}],"text":"Go has no exceptions for ordinary failures. A function that can fail returns an error as its last value, and the caller decides what to do. This feels repetitive for a day and then becomes a strength: every failure path is visible in the code, which is exactly what you want in software that runs production. This lesson covers the habits that make that style readable: wrapping, inspecting, and cleaning up with defer. What you will learn Functions with multiple return values Return, wrap and inspect errors with %w, errors.Is and errors.As Use defer for cleanup and understand its order When panic and recover are appropriate Errors are values func readConfig (path string ) (Config, error ) { data, err := os.ReadFile(path) if err != nil { return Config{}, fmt.Errorf( \"read config %s: %w\" , path, err) } var cfg Config if err := json.Unmarshal(data, &cfg); err != nil { return Config{}, fmt.Errorf( \"parse config %s: %w\" , path, err) } return cfg, nil } Check err != nil right after the call and return early. Add context with fmt.Errorf so the final message tells a story: read config /etc/app.json: open /etc/app.json: permission denied . The verb %w wraps the original error so it can still be inspected. Never ignore an error with _ unless you can explain why it cannot matter. Inspect errors _, err := os.Open( \"/etc/app.json\" ) if errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) { // use defaults } else if err != nil { return err } var pe *fs.PathError if errors.As(err, &pe) { fmt.Println( \"failed path:\" , pe.Path) } errors.Is compares an error (and everything it wraps) with a known value. errors.As finds an error of a specific type in the chain. You can define your own sentinel error with var ErrNoQuota = errors.New(\"no quota\") or your own type with an Error() string method. Do not compare error strings; they change. Defer defer schedules a call to run when the surrounding function returns, whatever the path. func copyFile (src, dst string ) error { in, err := os.Open(src) if err != nil { return err } defer in.Close() out, err := os.Create(dst) if err != nil { return err } defer out.Close() _, err = io.Copy(out, in) return err } Deferred calls run in last-in, first-out order. Arguments are evaluated when the defer statement runs, not when the call happens. Avoid defer inside a long loop, because the calls pile up until the function ends; move the body into a function instead. Deferred calls are how you release locks, close files, and stop timers without leaking them. Panic and recover panic stops normal flow and unwinds the stack. It is for programmer errors that should never happen (an impossible state, an index out of range) and not for \"file not found\". A web server framework usually calls recover in middleware so that one bad request does not kill the process, and logs the stack trace. In your own code, prefer returning errors. At the top of main , print a clear message to standard error and exit with a non-zero code so scripts and orchestrators see the failure: if err := run(); err != nil { fmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, \"error:\" , err) os.Exit( 1 ) } Predict first. A function returns `fmt.Errorf(\"open: %v\", err)` instead of using %w. What do you lose? Your guess Answer: The wrapped error. Callers can no longer use errors.Is or errors.As to detect the original cause. Use %w when the cause matters to callers. Incident: The error that said only \"failed\" Incident A nightly job logged one word and no one could tell what failed. 2026-01-05T02:00:07Z ERROR job failed $ grep -n \"return err\" jobs /sync.go | head -3 41: return err 58: return err 77: return errors.New(\"failed\") The code returned bare errors or a constant message, so the log had no hint about which step or file failed. The team adopted a rule: wrap every returned error with what the function was doing and the key identifier, and log once at the top. The same failure now reads \"sync users: fetch page 14: context deadline exceeded\", which points directly at the slow call. Common mistakes Ignoring error"},"go/interfaces-and-packages":{"html":"\u003cp>A tool that calls the real cloud API on every test is slow, flaky and expensive. The usual remedy is to depend on a small behaviour, not on a concrete client, and to substitute a fake in tests. Go does this with interfaces, which are satisfied implicitly: a type implements an interface just by having the methods. Together with a sensible package layout, this keeps programs easy to test and change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Define and satisfy interfaces implicitly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Accept interfaces, return concrete types\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Test with small fakes instead of real services\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Package layout, visibility and avoiding import cycles\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"implicit-interfaces\">Implicit interfaces\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An interface is a set of method signatures. Any type with those methods satisfies it; there is no \u003ccode>implements\u003c/code> keyword.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"type Notifier interface {\n    Send(msg string) error\n}\n\ntype Slack struct{ URL string }\n\nfunc (s Slack) Send(msg string) error {\n    // post to the webhook\n    return nil\n}\n\nfunc alert(n Notifier, msg string) error {\n    return n.Send(&quot;ALERT: &quot; + msg)\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">type\u003c/span> Notifier \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">interface\u003c/span> {\n    Send(msg \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">string\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">error\u003c/span>\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">type\u003c/span> Slack \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">struct\u003c/span>{ URL \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">string\u003c/span> }\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">(s Slack)\u003c/span>\u003c/span> Send(msg \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">string\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">error\u003c/span> {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">// post to the webhook\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">nil\u003c/span>\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title\">alert\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">(n Notifier, msg \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">string\u003c/span>)\u003c/span>\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">error\u003c/span> {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> n.Send(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ALERT: &quot;\u003c/span> + msg)\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>alert\u003c/code> works with Slack, email, a pager service or a test fake, without knowing which. Keep interfaces small, often one or two methods, such as \u003ccode>io.Reader\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>io.Writer\u003c/code> from the standard library. The wider an interface, the harder it is to fake and to satisfy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"accept-interfaces-return-structs\">Accept interfaces, return structs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A common guideline: functions \u003cstrong>accept\u003c/strong> the smallest interface they need and \u003cstrong>return\u003c/strong> concrete types. Define the interface where it is used (in the consumer package), not next to the implementation. That way the consumer owns the contract and the provider needs no knowledge of its callers. Do not create an interface for every struct &quot;just in case&quot;; add one when you have two implementations or when a test needs a fake.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"test-with-a-fake\">Test with a fake\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"type fakeNotifier struct{ sent []string }\n\nfunc (f *fakeNotifier) Send(msg string) error {\n    f.sent = append(f.sent, msg)\n    return nil\n}\n\nfunc TestAlertPrefix(t *testing.T) {\n    f := &amp;fakeNotifier{}\n    if err := alert(f, &quot;disk full&quot;); err != nil {\n        t.Fatal(err)\n    }\n    if got := f.sent[0]; got != &quot;ALERT: disk full&quot; {\n        t.Errorf(&quot;got %q&quot;, got)\n    }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">type\u003c/span> fakeNotifier \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">struct\u003c/span>{ sent []\u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">string\u003c/span> }\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">(f *fakeNotifier)\u003c/span>\u003c/span> Send(msg \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">string\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">error\u003c/span> {\n    f.sent = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">append\u003c/span>(f.sent, msg)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">nil\u003c/span>\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title\">TestAlertPrefix\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">(t *testing.T)\u003c/span>\u003c/span> {\n    f := &amp;fakeNotifier{}\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> err := alert(f, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;disk full&quot;\u003c/span>); err != \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">nil\u003c/span> {\n        t.Fatal(err)\n    }\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> got := f.sent[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>]; got != \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ALERT: disk full&quot;\u003c/span> {\n        t.Errorf(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;got %q&quot;\u003c/span>, got)\n    }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The test needs no network and runs in microseconds. For richer behaviour, the fake can return a configured error to check how your code handles failure, which is hard to provoke against a real service. The empty interface (\u003ccode>any\u003c/code>) accepts everything and checks nothing at compile time; use it sparingly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"packages\">Packages\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A package is a directory; all files in it share a name and a namespace. A typical layout for a tool:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"diskcheck/\n  go.mod\n  main.go            package main: parses flags, wires things together\n  internal/\n    probe/           package probe: reads disk usage\n    report/          package report: formats results\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">diskcheck/\n  go.mod\n  main.go            package main: parses flags, wires things together\n  internal/\n    probe/           package probe: reads disk usage\n    report/          package report: formats results\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Names starting with a capital letter are exported. The \u003ccode>internal\u003c/code> directory can only be imported by code inside the parent module, which keeps private code private. Go forbids \u003cstrong>import cycles\u003c/strong>, so if two packages need each other, pull the shared piece into a third package or reverse a dependency with an interface. Keep \u003ccode>main\u003c/code> thin: it reads configuration and calls into packages that are easy to test.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Your function calls a cloud client directly and tests fail whenever the network is down. What change fixes this?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Define a small interface for the behaviour you need, accept it as a parameter, and pass a fake in tests.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Depend on behaviour, not on a concrete service.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-tests-that-called-the-real-api\">Incident: The tests that called the real API\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A pipeline failed three times a week with timeouts, and no code had changed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">--- FAIL: TestListInstances (30.00s)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    inventory_test.go:21: Get &quot;https://ec2.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/&quot;: context deadline exceeded\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">FAIL\texample.com/ops/inventory\t30.019s\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The inventory package created a real cloud client inside its functions, so tests depended on credentials and network. The team introduced a one-method interface, Lister, passed it into the functions, and wrote a fake for tests. Unit tests now run in milliseconds and never touch the network; a small separate integration test, run nightly, checks the real API.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Creating an interface for every type\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Making interfaces large\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Importing concrete clients deep inside business logic\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Circular dependencies between packages\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"implicit-interfaces","text":"Implicit interfaces"},{"id":"accept-interfaces-return-structs","text":"Accept interfaces, return structs"},{"id":"test-with-a-fake","text":"Test with a fake"},{"id":"packages","text":"Packages"},{"id":"incident-the-tests-that-called-the-real-api","text":"Incident: The tests that called the real API"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Effective Go: Interfaces and other types","u":"https://go.dev/doc/effective_go#interfaces_and_types"},{"t":"How to write Go code: Organising code","u":"https://go.dev/doc/code"}],"text":"A tool that calls the real cloud API on every test is slow, flaky and expensive. The usual remedy is to depend on a small behaviour, not on a concrete client, and to substitute a fake in tests. Go does this with interfaces, which are satisfied implicitly: a type implements an interface just by having the methods. Together with a sensible package layout, this keeps programs easy to test and change. What you will learn Define and satisfy interfaces implicitly Accept interfaces, return concrete types Test with small fakes instead of real services Package layout, visibility and avoiding import cycles Implicit interfaces An interface is a set of method signatures. Any type with those methods satisfies it; there is no implements keyword. type Notifier interface { Send(msg string ) error } type Slack struct { URL string } func (s Slack) Send(msg string ) error { // post to the webhook return nil } func alert (n Notifier, msg string ) error { return n.Send( \"ALERT: \" + msg) } alert works with Slack, email, a pager service or a test fake, without knowing which. Keep interfaces small, often one or two methods, such as io.Reader and io.Writer from the standard library. The wider an interface, the harder it is to fake and to satisfy. Accept interfaces, return structs A common guideline: functions accept the smallest interface they need and return concrete types. Define the interface where it is used (in the consumer package), not next to the implementation. That way the consumer owns the contract and the provider needs no knowledge of its callers. Do not create an interface for every struct \"just in case\"; add one when you have two implementations or when a test needs a fake. Test with a fake type fakeNotifier struct { sent [] string } func (f *fakeNotifier) Send(msg string ) error { f.sent = append (f.sent, msg) return nil } func TestAlertPrefix (t *testing.T) { f := &fakeNotifier{} if err := alert(f, \"disk full\" ); err != nil { t.Fatal(err) } if got := f.sent[ 0 ]; got != \"ALERT: disk full\" { t.Errorf( \"got %q\" , got) } } The test needs no network and runs in microseconds. For richer behaviour, the fake can return a configured error to check how your code handles failure, which is hard to provoke against a real service. The empty interface ( any ) accepts everything and checks nothing at compile time; use it sparingly. Packages A package is a directory; all files in it share a name and a namespace. A typical layout for a tool: diskcheck/ go.mod main.go package main: parses flags, wires things together internal/ probe/ package probe: reads disk usage report/ package report: formats results Names starting with a capital letter are exported. The internal directory can only be imported by code inside the parent module, which keeps private code private. Go forbids import cycles , so if two packages need each other, pull the shared piece into a third package or reverse a dependency with an interface. Keep main thin: it reads configuration and calls into packages that are easy to test. Predict first. Your function calls a cloud client directly and tests fail whenever the network is down. What change fixes this? Your guess Answer: Define a small interface for the behaviour you need, accept it as a parameter, and pass a fake in tests. Depend on behaviour, not on a concrete service. Incident: The tests that called the real API Incident A pipeline failed three times a week with timeouts, and no code had changed. --- FAIL: TestListInstances (30.00s) inventory_test.go:21: Get \"https://ec2.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/\": context deadline exceeded FAIL example.com/ops/inventory 30.019s The inventory package created a real cloud client inside its functions, so tests depended on credentials and network. The team introduced a one-method interface, Lister, passed it into the functions, and wrote a fake for tests. Unit tests now run in milliseconds and never touch the network; a small separate integration test, run nightly, checks the real API. Common mistakes "},"go/goroutines-channels-and-sync":{"html":"\u003cp>An operations tool often checks many things at once: a hundred hosts, a dozen endpoints, a queue of files. Done one by one, it takes minutes; done concurrently, seconds. Go makes concurrency cheap with goroutines, but concurrency also creates a new class of bugs: leaks, deadlocks and data races. A few patterns and one tool, the race detector, handle most of the risk.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Start goroutines and wait for them with sync.WaitGroup\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pass results with channels and know when to close them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Protect shared data with sync.Mutex or avoid sharing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Bound concurrency with a worker pool and detect races with -race\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"goroutines-and-waitgroup\">Goroutines and WaitGroup\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A goroutine is a lightweight thread started with the \u003ccode>go\u003c/code> keyword. The program exits when \u003ccode>main\u003c/code> returns, even if goroutines are still running, so you must wait for them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"var wg sync.WaitGroup\nfor _, h := range hosts {\n    wg.Add(1)\n    go func(host string) {\n        defer wg.Done()\n        check(host)\n    }(h)\n}\nwg.Wait()\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">var\u003c/span> wg sync.WaitGroup\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> _, h := \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">range\u003c/span> hosts {\n    wg.Add(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">go\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">(host \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">string\u003c/span>)\u003c/span>\u003c/span> {\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">defer\u003c/span> wg.Done()\n        check(host)\n    }(h)\n}\nwg.Wait()\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Pass the loop variable as an argument (or rely on Go 1.22 and later, where each iteration has its own variable). In older code, capturing \u003ccode>h\u003c/code> directly meant that every goroutine saw the last host, a classic bug.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"channels\">Channels\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A channel carries values between goroutines and synchronises them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"results := make(chan Result)\nfor _, h := range hosts {\n    go func() { results &lt;- probe(h) }()\n}\nfor range hosts {\n    r := &lt;-results\n    fmt.Println(r.Host, r.OK)\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">results := \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">make\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">chan\u003c/span> Result)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> _, h := \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">range\u003c/span> hosts {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">go\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">()\u003c/span>\u003c/span> { results &lt;- probe(h) }()\n}\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">range\u003c/span> hosts {\n    r := &lt;-results\n    fmt.Println(r.Host, r.OK)\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>An unbuffered channel blocks the sender until a receiver is ready; a buffered channel (\u003ccode>make(chan T, 10)\u003c/code>) blocks only when full. The \u003cstrong>sender\u003c/strong> closes a channel to say &quot;no more values&quot;, and receivers can \u003ccode>range\u003c/code> over it until it closes. Sending on a closed channel panics, and a goroutine that blocks forever on a channel nobody reads is a \u003cstrong>leak\u003c/strong>, which shows up as slowly growing memory.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"shared-state\">Shared state\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If goroutines must share a variable, protect it:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"type Counter struct {\n    mu sync.Mutex\n    n  map[string]int\n}\n\nfunc (c *Counter) Inc(k string) {\n    c.mu.Lock()\n    defer c.mu.Unlock()\n    c.n[k]++\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">type\u003c/span> Counter \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">struct\u003c/span> {\n    mu sync.Mutex\n    n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">map\u003c/span>[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">string\u003c/span>]\u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">int\u003c/span>\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">(c *Counter)\u003c/span>\u003c/span> Inc(k \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">string\u003c/span>) {\n    c.mu.Lock()\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">defer\u003c/span> c.mu.Unlock()\n    c.n[k]++\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A plain map written by two goroutines is a \u003cstrong>data race\u003c/strong> and can crash with &quot;concurrent map writes&quot;. Use a mutex, \u003ccode>sync/atomic\u003c/code> for simple counters, or better, give one goroutine ownership of the data and send it messages. The saying goes: share memory by communicating, not communicate by sharing memory.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limit-concurrency-and-test-for-races\">Limit concurrency and test for races\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Starting 50,000 goroutines that each open a connection will exhaust file descriptors or overload the target. Use a bounded worker pool:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"jobs := make(chan string)\nvar wg sync.WaitGroup\nfor i := 0; i &lt; 8; i++ {\n    wg.Add(1)\n    go func() {\n        defer wg.Done()\n        for h := range jobs {\n            check(h)\n        }\n    }()\n}\nfor _, h := range hosts {\n    jobs &lt;- h\n}\nclose(jobs)\nwg.Wait()\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">jobs := \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">make\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">chan\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">string\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">var\u003c/span> wg sync.WaitGroup\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> i := \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>; i &lt; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8\u003c/span>; i++ {\n    wg.Add(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">go\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">()\u003c/span>\u003c/span> {\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">defer\u003c/span> wg.Done()\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> h := \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">range\u003c/span> jobs {\n            check(h)\n        }\n    }()\n}\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> _, h := \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">range\u003c/span> hosts {\n    jobs &lt;- h\n}\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">close\u003c/span>(jobs)\nwg.Wait()\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Run tests with \u003ccode>go test -race ./...\u003c/code> and, in CI, build a test binary with the race detector. It reports unsynchronised access with stack traces of both goroutines. It slows the program, so use it in tests and staging, not in production.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Your tool starts one goroutine per file for 200,000 files and runs out of file descriptors. What is the standard fix?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Use a bounded worker pool, a fixed number of goroutines reading jobs from a channel.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Concurrency needs a limit that matches resources.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-concurrent-map-writes-in-production\">Incident: Concurrent map writes in production\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A health aggregator crashed every few days with a fatal error and no panic message to recover from.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">fatal error: concurrent map writes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">goroutine 412 [running]:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">runtime.throw({0x9f1a2c, 0x15})\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">main.(*Aggregator).record(...)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    /src/agg.go:57\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Several probe goroutines wrote to the same map without a lock. The race detector found it immediately when the team ran the tests with -race, which they had never done. A mutex around the map fixed the crash, and CI now runs go test -race on every pull request. They also bounded the number of probes in flight.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Forgetting to wait for goroutines\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Closing a channel from the receiving side\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Unbounded goroutines\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sharing maps without locks\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"goroutines-and-waitgroup","text":"Goroutines and WaitGroup"},{"id":"channels","text":"Channels"},{"id":"shared-state","text":"Shared state"},{"id":"limit-concurrency-and-test-for-races","text":"Limit concurrency and test for races"},{"id":"incident-concurrent-map-writes-in-production","text":"Incident: Concurrent map writes in production"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Go blog: Share Memory By Communicating","u":"https://go.dev/blog/codelab-share"},{"t":"Data Race Detector","u":"https://go.dev/doc/articles/race_detector"}],"text":"An operations tool often checks many things at once: a hundred hosts, a dozen endpoints, a queue of files. Done one by one, it takes minutes; done concurrently, seconds. Go makes concurrency cheap with goroutines, but concurrency also creates a new class of bugs: leaks, deadlocks and data races. A few patterns and one tool, the race detector, handle most of the risk. What you will learn Start goroutines and wait for them with sync.WaitGroup Pass results with channels and know when to close them Protect shared data with sync.Mutex or avoid sharing Bound concurrency with a worker pool and detect races with -race Goroutines and WaitGroup A goroutine is a lightweight thread started with the go keyword. The program exits when main returns, even if goroutines are still running, so you must wait for them. var wg sync.WaitGroup for _, h := range hosts { wg.Add( 1 ) go func (host string ) { defer wg.Done() check(host) }(h) } wg.Wait() Pass the loop variable as an argument (or rely on Go 1.22 and later, where each iteration has its own variable). In older code, capturing h directly meant that every goroutine saw the last host, a classic bug. Channels A channel carries values between goroutines and synchronises them. results := make ( chan Result) for _, h := range hosts { go func () { results \u003c- probe(h) }() } for range hosts { r := \u003c-results fmt.Println(r.Host, r.OK) } An unbuffered channel blocks the sender until a receiver is ready; a buffered channel ( make(chan T, 10) ) blocks only when full. The sender closes a channel to say \"no more values\", and receivers can range over it until it closes. Sending on a closed channel panics, and a goroutine that blocks forever on a channel nobody reads is a leak , which shows up as slowly growing memory. Shared state If goroutines must share a variable, protect it: type Counter struct { mu sync.Mutex n map [ string ] int } func (c *Counter) Inc(k string ) { c.mu.Lock() defer c.mu.Unlock() c.n[k]++ } A plain map written by two goroutines is a data race and can crash with \"concurrent map writes\". Use a mutex, sync/atomic for simple counters, or better, give one goroutine ownership of the data and send it messages. The saying goes: share memory by communicating, not communicate by sharing memory. Limit concurrency and test for races Starting 50,000 goroutines that each open a connection will exhaust file descriptors or overload the target. Use a bounded worker pool: jobs := make ( chan string ) var wg sync.WaitGroup for i := 0 ; i \u003c 8 ; i++ { wg.Add( 1 ) go func () { defer wg.Done() for h := range jobs { check(h) } }() } for _, h := range hosts { jobs \u003c- h } close (jobs) wg.Wait() Run tests with go test -race ./... and, in CI, build a test binary with the race detector. It reports unsynchronised access with stack traces of both goroutines. It slows the program, so use it in tests and staging, not in production. Predict first. Your tool starts one goroutine per file for 200,000 files and runs out of file descriptors. What is the standard fix? Your guess Answer: Use a bounded worker pool, a fixed number of goroutines reading jobs from a channel. Concurrency needs a limit that matches resources. Incident: Concurrent map writes in production Incident A health aggregator crashed every few days with a fatal error and no panic message to recover from. fatal error: concurrent map writes goroutine 412 [running]: runtime.throw({0x9f1a2c, 0x15}) main.(*Aggregator).record(...) /src/agg.go:57 Several probe goroutines wrote to the same map without a lock. The race detector found it immediately when the team ran the tests with -race, which they had never done. A mutex around the map fixed the crash, and CI now runs go test -race on every pull request. They also bounded the number of probes in flight. Common mistakes Forgetting to wait for goroutines Closing a channel from the receiving side Unbounded goroutines Sharing maps without locks"},"go/context-timeouts-and-cancellation":{"html":"\u003cp>The most common reliability bug in network code is a call that waits forever. A dependency slows down, every request piles up behind it, and the whole service stalls. Go&#39;s answer is the context package: a value passed down the call chain that carries a deadline, a cancellation signal and a few request-scoped values. Using it consistently is what separates a toy program from a service you can operate.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Create contexts with timeouts and deadlines\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pass ctx as the first argument and respect it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Set client and server timeouts in net/http\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Shut down gracefully on SIGTERM\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"contexts\">Contexts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A context is created from a parent and can be cancelled. Cancelling the parent cancels all children.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 2*time.Second)\ndefer cancel()\n\nreq, err := http.NewRequestWithContext(ctx, http.MethodGet, url, nil)\nif err != nil {\n    return err\n}\nresp, err := http.DefaultClient.Do(req)\nif err != nil {\n    return fmt.Errorf(&quot;get %s: %w&quot;, url, err)  // may be context deadline exceeded\n}\ndefer resp.Body.Close()\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>*time.Second)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">defer\u003c/span> cancel()\n\nreq, err := http.NewRequestWithContext(ctx, http.MethodGet, url, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">nil\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> err != \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">nil\u003c/span> {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> err\n}\nresp, err := http.DefaultClient.Do(req)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> err != \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">nil\u003c/span> {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> fmt.Errorf(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;get %s: %w&quot;\u003c/span>, url, err)  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">// may be context deadline exceeded\u003c/span>\n}\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">defer\u003c/span> resp.Body.Close()\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Always call \u003ccode>cancel\u003c/code>, usually with \u003ccode>defer\u003c/code>, to release resources. The convention is that \u003ccode>ctx context.Context\u003c/code> is the first parameter of any function that does I/O or may block, and it is passed along, not stored in structs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"respect-cancellation-in-your-own-code\">Respect cancellation in your own code\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Loops and waits must check the context, or cancellation does nothing:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"func poll(ctx context.Context, every time.Duration, f func() error) error {\n    t := time.NewTicker(every)\n    defer t.Stop()\n    for {\n        select {\n        case &lt;-ctx.Done():\n            return ctx.Err()   // context.Canceled or DeadlineExceeded\n        case &lt;-t.C:\n            if err := f(); err != nil {\n                return err\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title\">poll\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">(ctx context.Context, every time.Duration, f \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span>()\u003c/span>\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">error\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">error\u003c/span> {\n    t := time.NewTicker(every)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">defer\u003c/span> t.Stop()\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> {\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">select\u003c/span> {\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">case\u003c/span> &lt;-ctx.Done():\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> ctx.Err()   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">// context.Canceled or DeadlineExceeded\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">case\u003c/span> &lt;-t.C:\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> err := f(); err != \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">nil\u003c/span> {\n                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> err\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>select\u003c/code> waits on several channels and takes the first that is ready. A goroutine that never looks at \u003ccode>ctx.Done()\u003c/code> is the usual cause of leaks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"timeouts-in-net-http\">Timeouts in net/http\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The default \u003ccode>http.Client\u003c/code> has \u003cstrong>no timeout\u003c/strong>, and a default server has none for slow clients. Set them explicitly:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"client := &amp;http.Client{Timeout: 10 * time.Second}\n\nsrv := &amp;http.Server{\n    Addr:              &quot;:8080&quot;,\n    Handler:           mux,\n    ReadHeaderTimeout: 5 * time.Second,\n    ReadTimeout:       15 * time.Second,\n    WriteTimeout:      30 * time.Second,\n    IdleTimeout:       60 * time.Second,\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">client := &amp;http.Client{Timeout: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span> * time.Second}\n\nsrv := &amp;http.Server{\n    Addr:              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;:8080&quot;\u003c/span>,\n    Handler:           mux,\n    ReadHeaderTimeout: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span> * time.Second,\n    ReadTimeout:       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">15\u003c/span> * time.Second,\n    WriteTimeout:      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30\u003c/span> * time.Second,\n    IdleTimeout:       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">60\u003c/span> * time.Second,\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Choose timeouts from what your callers can tolerate, and keep inner timeouts shorter than outer ones so the outer caller still has time to retry or fail cleanly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"graceful-shutdown\">Graceful shutdown\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When Kubernetes or systemd stops your service, it sends SIGTERM. A graceful service stops accepting new requests, finishes the ones in flight, then exits.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ctx, stop := signal.NotifyContext(context.Background(), os.Interrupt, syscall.SIGTERM)\ndefer stop()\n\ngo func() { _ = srv.ListenAndServe() }()\n\n&lt;-ctx.Done()\nshutdownCtx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 20*time.Second)\ndefer cancel()\nif err := srv.Shutdown(shutdownCtx); err != nil {\n    log.Printf(&quot;forced shutdown: %v&quot;, err)\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ctx, stop := signal.NotifyContext(context.Background(), os.Interrupt, syscall.SIGTERM)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">defer\u003c/span> stop()\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">go\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">()\u003c/span>\u003c/span> { _ = srv.ListenAndServe() }()\n\n&lt;-ctx.Done()\nshutdownCtx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">20\u003c/span>*time.Second)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">defer\u003c/span> cancel()\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> err := srv.Shutdown(shutdownCtx); err != \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">nil\u003c/span> {\n    log.Printf(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;forced shutdown: %v&quot;\u003c/span>, err)\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Make the shutdown timeout shorter than the orchestrator&#39;s grace period (30 seconds by default in Kubernetes), or the process will be killed in the middle of the work.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Your service calls a slow dependency without a timeout. What happens under load, and what is the fix?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Requests pile up waiting, goroutines and connections accumulate, and the service stalls. Use a context with a timeout (and client timeouts) on every outbound call.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A missing timeout turns one slow dependency into an outage.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-service-that-never-finished-a-deploy\">Incident: The service that never finished a deploy\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each rollout took ten minutes and ended with dropped requests.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl rollout status deploy/payments\nkubectl logs payments-7c9 --previous | tail -2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout status deploy/payments\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Waiting for deployment &quot;payments&quot; rollout to finish: 1 old replicas are pending termination...\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs payments-7c9 --previous | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2026-01-05T11:02:41Z listening on :8080\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The program ignored SIGTERM, so Kubernetes waited the full 30-second grace period and then killed it, dropping open requests. The team added signal.NotifyContext and srv.Shutdown with a 20-second limit, plus a readiness check that turns false at shutdown start. Rollouts now finish in a minute with no errors.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using http.DefaultClient with no timeout\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Storing contexts in structs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never calling cancel\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Shutdown timeouts longer than the grace period\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"contexts","text":"Contexts"},{"id":"respect-cancellation-in-your-own-code","text":"Respect cancellation in your own code"},{"id":"timeouts-in-net-http","text":"Timeouts in net/http"},{"id":"graceful-shutdown","text":"Graceful shutdown"},{"id":"incident-the-service-that-never-finished-a-deploy","text":"Incident: The service that never finished a deploy"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Go blog: Go Concurrency Patterns: Context","u":"https://go.dev/blog/context"},{"t":"net/http package documentation","u":"https://pkg.go.dev/net/http"}],"text":"The most common reliability bug in network code is a call that waits forever. A dependency slows down, every request piles up behind it, and the whole service stalls. Go&#39;s answer is the context package: a value passed down the call chain that carries a deadline, a cancellation signal and a few request-scoped values. Using it consistently is what separates a toy program from a service you can operate. What you will learn Create contexts with timeouts and deadlines Pass ctx as the first argument and respect it Set client and server timeouts in net/http Shut down gracefully on SIGTERM Contexts A context is created from a parent and can be cancelled. Cancelling the parent cancels all children. ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 2 *time.Second) defer cancel() req, err := http.NewRequestWithContext(ctx, http.MethodGet, url, nil ) if err != nil { return err } resp, err := http.DefaultClient.Do(req) if err != nil { return fmt.Errorf( \"get %s: %w\" , url, err) // may be context deadline exceeded } defer resp.Body.Close() Always call cancel , usually with defer , to release resources. The convention is that ctx context.Context is the first parameter of any function that does I/O or may block, and it is passed along, not stored in structs. Respect cancellation in your own code Loops and waits must check the context, or cancellation does nothing: func poll (ctx context.Context, every time.Duration, f func () error ) error { t := time.NewTicker(every) defer t.Stop() for { select { case \u003c-ctx.Done(): return ctx.Err() // context.Canceled or DeadlineExceeded case \u003c-t.C: if err := f(); err != nil { return err } } } } select waits on several channels and takes the first that is ready. A goroutine that never looks at ctx.Done() is the usual cause of leaks. Timeouts in net/http The default http.Client has no timeout , and a default server has none for slow clients. Set them explicitly: client := &http.Client{Timeout: 10 * time.Second} srv := &http.Server{ Addr: \":8080\" , Handler: mux, ReadHeaderTimeout: 5 * time.Second, ReadTimeout: 15 * time.Second, WriteTimeout: 30 * time.Second, IdleTimeout: 60 * time.Second, } Choose timeouts from what your callers can tolerate, and keep inner timeouts shorter than outer ones so the outer caller still has time to retry or fail cleanly. Graceful shutdown When Kubernetes or systemd stops your service, it sends SIGTERM. A graceful service stops accepting new requests, finishes the ones in flight, then exits. ctx, stop := signal.NotifyContext(context.Background(), os.Interrupt, syscall.SIGTERM) defer stop() go func () { _ = srv.ListenAndServe() }() \u003c-ctx.Done() shutdownCtx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 20 *time.Second) defer cancel() if err := srv.Shutdown(shutdownCtx); err != nil { log.Printf( \"forced shutdown: %v\" , err) } Make the shutdown timeout shorter than the orchestrator&#39;s grace period (30 seconds by default in Kubernetes), or the process will be killed in the middle of the work. Predict first. Your service calls a slow dependency without a timeout. What happens under load, and what is the fix? Your guess Answer: Requests pile up waiting, goroutines and connections accumulate, and the service stalls. Use a context with a timeout (and client timeouts) on every outbound call. A missing timeout turns one slow dependency into an outage. Incident: The service that never finished a deploy Incident Each rollout took ten minutes and ended with dropped requests. $ kubectl rollout status deploy/payments Waiting for deployment \"payments\" rollout to finish: 1 old replicas are pending termination... $ kubectl logs payments-7c9 --previous | tail -2 2026-01-05T11:02:41Z listening on :8080 The program ignored SIGTERM, so Kubernetes waited the full 30-second grace period and then killed it, dropping open requests. The team added signal.NotifyContext and srv.Shutdown with a 20-second limit, plus a readiness check that turns false at shutdown start. Rollouts now finish in"},"go/http-services-and-json":{"html":"\u003cp>Much operations work is an API: a webhook receiver, a metrics exporter, a small internal service that Kubernetes probes and other teams call. The Go standard library is enough to build these without a framework. This lesson shows the shape of a clean service: routing, JSON in and out, a health endpoint, a bit of middleware, and a client that behaves well.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Write handlers and route requests with http.ServeMux\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Encode and decode JSON with struct tags and validation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Add middleware for logging and recovery\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expose health endpoints and call other services with a tuned client\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"handlers-and-routing\">Handlers and routing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"mux := http.NewServeMux()\nmux.HandleFunc(&quot;GET /healthz&quot;, func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {\n    w.WriteHeader(http.StatusOK)\n    fmt.Fprintln(w, &quot;ok&quot;)\n})\nmux.HandleFunc(&quot;GET /hosts/{name}&quot;, getHost)\nmux.HandleFunc(&quot;POST /hosts&quot;, createHost)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">mux := http.NewServeMux()\nmux.HandleFunc(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;GET /healthz&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)\u003c/span>\u003c/span> {\n    w.WriteHeader(http.StatusOK)\n    fmt.Fprintln(w, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ok&quot;\u003c/span>)\n})\nmux.HandleFunc(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;GET /hosts/{name}&quot;\u003c/span>, getHost)\nmux.HandleFunc(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;POST /hosts&quot;\u003c/span>, createHost)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Since Go 1.22 the standard router understands methods and path variables like \u003ccode>{name}\u003c/code>, read with \u003ccode>r.PathValue(&quot;name&quot;)\u003c/code>. A handler receives the response writer and the request, and you set headers and status before writing the body. Return an appropriate code: 400 for bad input, 404 for missing, 500 for your own bugs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"json-in-and-out\">JSON in and out\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"type Host struct {\n    Name string `json:&quot;name&quot;`\n    Port int    `json:&quot;port&quot;`\n}\n\nfunc createHost(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {\n    r.Body = http.MaxBytesReader(w, r.Body, 1&lt;&lt;20)\n    var h Host\n    dec := json.NewDecoder(r.Body)\n    dec.DisallowUnknownFields()\n    if err := dec.Decode(&amp;h); err != nil {\n        http.Error(w, &quot;bad json: &quot;+err.Error(), http.StatusBadRequest)\n        return\n    }\n    if h.Name == &quot;&quot; || h.Port &lt; 1 || h.Port &gt; 65535 {\n        http.Error(w, &quot;name and a valid port are required&quot;, http.StatusBadRequest)\n        return\n    }\n    w.Header().Set(&quot;Content-Type&quot;, &quot;application/json&quot;)\n    w.WriteHeader(http.StatusCreated)\n    _ = json.NewEncoder(w).Encode(h)\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">type\u003c/span> Host \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">struct\u003c/span> {\n    Name \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">string\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">`json:&quot;name&quot;`\u003c/span>\n    Port \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">int\u003c/span>    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">`json:&quot;port&quot;`\u003c/span>\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title\">createHost\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)\u003c/span>\u003c/span> {\n    r.Body = http.MaxBytesReader(w, r.Body, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>&lt;&lt;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">20\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">var\u003c/span> h Host\n    dec := json.NewDecoder(r.Body)\n    dec.DisallowUnknownFields()\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> err := dec.Decode(&amp;h); err != \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">nil\u003c/span> {\n        http.Error(w, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;bad json: &quot;\u003c/span>+err.Error(), http.StatusBadRequest)\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span>\n    }\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> h.Name == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;\u003c/span> || h.Port &lt; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span> || h.Port &gt; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">65535\u003c/span> {\n        http.Error(w, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name and a valid port are required&quot;\u003c/span>, http.StatusBadRequest)\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span>\n    }\n    w.Header().Set(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Content-Type&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;application/json&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    w.WriteHeader(http.StatusCreated)\n    _ = json.NewEncoder(w).Encode(h)\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Limit the body size, reject unknown fields if your API is strict, and validate the values, because decoding only checks the shape. Struct tags name the fields; \u003ccode>omitempty\u003c/code> skips empty ones.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"middleware\">Middleware\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Middleware wraps a handler to add behaviour such as logging or panic recovery.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"func logging(next http.Handler) http.Handler {\n    return http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {\n        start := time.Now()\n        next.ServeHTTP(w, r)\n        log.Printf(&quot;%s %s %s&quot;, r.Method, r.URL.Path, time.Since(start))\n    })\n}\n\nsrv := &amp;http.Server{Addr: &quot;:8080&quot;, Handler: logging(mux)}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title\">logging\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">(next http.Handler)\u003c/span>\u003c/span> http.Handler {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> http.HandlerFunc(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)\u003c/span>\u003c/span> {\n        start := time.Now()\n        next.ServeHTTP(w, r)\n        log.Printf(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;%s %s %s&quot;\u003c/span>, r.Method, r.URL.Path, time.Since(start))\n    })\n}\n\nsrv := &amp;http.Server{Addr: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;:8080&quot;\u003c/span>, Handler: logging(mux)}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Chain them for recovery, request IDs, authentication and metrics. Use structured logging (\u003ccode>log/slog\u003c/code>) so fields such as the request ID can be searched.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"health-endpoints-and-clients\">Health endpoints and clients\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Expose two endpoints: \u003cstrong>liveness\u003c/strong> (\u003ccode>/healthz\u003c/code>, the process is not stuck) and \u003cstrong>readiness\u003c/strong> (\u003ccode>/readyz\u003c/code>, it can take traffic: database reachable, caches warm). Kubernetes probes use them. Keep liveness cheap and do not make it depend on the database, or a database blip will restart every pod.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>For outbound calls, reuse one \u003ccode>http.Client\u003c/code> (it pools connections), set \u003ccode>Timeout\u003c/code>, always close \u003ccode>resp.Body\u003c/code>, check the status code, and retry only idempotent requests with backoff and a limit. Never build a new client per request; it leaks connections and sockets.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Liveness probes call the database. The database restarts for one minute. What happens to your pods?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> They fail liveness and are restarted, turning a short database blip into an outage. Keep liveness independent and use readiness for dependencies.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Probes encode what a failure means.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-fresh-client-for-each-request\">Incident: A fresh client for each request\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A collector service slowly ran out of sockets and began failing with connection errors.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ss -tan | grep -c TIME-WAIT\ngrep -n &quot;http.Client{&quot; collector/*.go\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">dial tcp 10.2.4.7:443: connect: cannot assign requested address\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ss -tan | grep -c TIME-WAIT\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">61204\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;http.Client{&quot;\u003c/span> collector/*.go\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">collector/fetch.go:31:\tc := &amp;http.Client{}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each fetch created a new client with its own transport, so connections were never reused and thousands sat in TIME-WAIT. The team created one shared client with a timeout and a tuned transport, closed response bodies in all code paths, and added a metric for open connections. Socket use dropped by 99 percent.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No body size limit on requests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping validation after decoding\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Creating a client per request\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Liveness probes that check dependencies\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"handlers-and-routing","text":"Handlers and routing"},{"id":"json-in-and-out","text":"JSON in and out"},{"id":"middleware","text":"Middleware"},{"id":"health-endpoints-and-clients","text":"Health endpoints and clients"},{"id":"incident-a-fresh-client-for-each-request","text":"Incident: A fresh client for each request"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Writing Web Applications","u":"https://go.dev/doc/articles/wiki/"},{"t":"encoding/json package","u":"https://pkg.go.dev/encoding/json"}],"text":"Much operations work is an API: a webhook receiver, a metrics exporter, a small internal service that Kubernetes probes and other teams call. The Go standard library is enough to build these without a framework. This lesson shows the shape of a clean service: routing, JSON in and out, a health endpoint, a bit of middleware, and a client that behaves well. What you will learn Write handlers and route requests with http.ServeMux Encode and decode JSON with struct tags and validation Add middleware for logging and recovery Expose health endpoints and call other services with a tuned client Handlers and routing mux := http.NewServeMux() mux.HandleFunc( \"GET /healthz\" , func (w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) { w.WriteHeader(http.StatusOK) fmt.Fprintln(w, \"ok\" ) }) mux.HandleFunc( \"GET /hosts/{name}\" , getHost) mux.HandleFunc( \"POST /hosts\" , createHost) Since Go 1.22 the standard router understands methods and path variables like {name} , read with r.PathValue(\"name\") . A handler receives the response writer and the request, and you set headers and status before writing the body. Return an appropriate code: 400 for bad input, 404 for missing, 500 for your own bugs. JSON in and out type Host struct { Name string `json:\"name\"` Port int `json:\"port\"` } func createHost (w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) { r.Body = http.MaxBytesReader(w, r.Body, 1 \u003c\u003c 20 ) var h Host dec := json.NewDecoder(r.Body) dec.DisallowUnknownFields() if err := dec.Decode(&h); err != nil { http.Error(w, \"bad json: \" +err.Error(), http.StatusBadRequest) return } if h.Name == \"\" || h.Port \u003c 1 || h.Port > 65535 { http.Error(w, \"name and a valid port are required\" , http.StatusBadRequest) return } w.Header().Set( \"Content-Type\" , \"application/json\" ) w.WriteHeader(http.StatusCreated) _ = json.NewEncoder(w).Encode(h) } Limit the body size, reject unknown fields if your API is strict, and validate the values, because decoding only checks the shape. Struct tags name the fields; omitempty skips empty ones. Middleware Middleware wraps a handler to add behaviour such as logging or panic recovery. func logging (next http.Handler) http.Handler { return http.HandlerFunc( func (w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) { start := time.Now() next.ServeHTTP(w, r) log.Printf( \"%s %s %s\" , r.Method, r.URL.Path, time.Since(start)) }) } srv := &http.Server{Addr: \":8080\" , Handler: logging(mux)} Chain them for recovery, request IDs, authentication and metrics. Use structured logging ( log/slog ) so fields such as the request ID can be searched. Health endpoints and clients Expose two endpoints: liveness ( /healthz , the process is not stuck) and readiness ( /readyz , it can take traffic: database reachable, caches warm). Kubernetes probes use them. Keep liveness cheap and do not make it depend on the database, or a database blip will restart every pod. For outbound calls, reuse one http.Client (it pools connections), set Timeout , always close resp.Body , check the status code, and retry only idempotent requests with backoff and a limit. Never build a new client per request; it leaks connections and sockets. Predict first. Liveness probes call the database. The database restarts for one minute. What happens to your pods? Your guess Answer: They fail liveness and are restarted, turning a short database blip into an outage. Keep liveness independent and use readiness for dependencies. Probes encode what a failure means. Incident: A fresh client for each request Incident A collector service slowly ran out of sockets and began failing with connection errors. dial tcp 10.2.4.7:443: connect: cannot assign requested address $ ss -tan | grep -c TIME-WAIT 61204 $ grep -n \"http.Client{\" collector/*.go collector/fetch.go:31: c := &http.Client{} Each fetch created a new client with its own transport, so connections were never reused and thousands sat in TIME-WAIT. The team created one shared client with a timeout and a tuned transport, closed response bodies in all code paths, and adde"},"go/testing-benchmarks-and-tooling":{"html":"\u003cp>Go has testing built in: a file ending in _test.go, a function starting with Test, and the go test command. There is no framework to choose or install. Combined with the race detector, benchmarks and vet, you get a solid safety net with very little setup, which is why Go projects are easy to contribute to and safe to change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Write table-driven unit tests with subtests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use test helpers, temporary directories and fakes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Measure coverage and write benchmarks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Run vet, race and linters in CI\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-first-test\">A first test\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Tests live next to the code in \u003ccode>_test.go\u003c/code> files.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"// parse.go\nfunc ParseSize(s string) (int64, error) { /* ... */ }\n\n// parse_test.go\nfunc TestParseSize(t *testing.T) {\n    tests := []struct {\n        name    string\n        in      string\n        want    int64\n        wantErr bool\n    }{\n        {&quot;bytes&quot;, &quot;512&quot;, 512, false},\n        {&quot;kilobytes&quot;, &quot;2K&quot;, 2048, false},\n        {&quot;empty&quot;, &quot;&quot;, 0, true},\n        {&quot;junk&quot;, &quot;12Q&quot;, 0, true},\n    }\n    for _, tc := range tests {\n        t.Run(tc.name, func(t *testing.T) {\n            got, err := ParseSize(tc.in)\n            if (err != nil) != tc.wantErr {\n                t.Fatalf(&quot;err = %v, wantErr %v&quot;, err, tc.wantErr)\n            }\n            if got != tc.want {\n                t.Errorf(&quot;got %d, want %d&quot;, got, tc.want)\n            }\n        })\n    }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">// parse.go\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title\">ParseSize\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">(s \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">string\u003c/span>)\u003c/span>\u003c/span> (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">int64\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">error\u003c/span>) { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">/* ... */\u003c/span> }\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">// parse_test.go\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title\">TestParseSize\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">(t *testing.T)\u003c/span>\u003c/span> {\n    tests := []\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">struct\u003c/span> {\n        name    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">string\u003c/span>\n        in      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">string\u003c/span>\n        want    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">int64\u003c/span>\n        wantErr \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">bool\u003c/span>\n    }{\n        {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;bytes&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;512&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">512\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>},\n        {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;kilobytes&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2K&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2048\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>},\n        {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;empty&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>},\n        {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;junk&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;12Q&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>},\n    }\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> _, tc := \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">range\u003c/span> tests {\n        t.Run(tc.name, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">(t *testing.T)\u003c/span>\u003c/span> {\n            got, err := ParseSize(tc.in)\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> (err != \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">nil\u003c/span>) != tc.wantErr {\n                t.Fatalf(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;err = %v, wantErr %v&quot;\u003c/span>, err, tc.wantErr)\n            }\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> got != tc.want {\n                t.Errorf(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;got %d, want %d&quot;\u003c/span>, got, tc.want)\n            }\n        })\n    }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>table-driven\u003c/strong> style makes adding a case one line and gives each case a name in the output.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"helpers-and-temporary-files\">Helpers and temporary files\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>t.TempDir()\u003c/code> gives a directory that is cleaned up after the test. \u003ccode>t.Helper()\u003c/code> marks a function as a helper so failures point at the caller. \u003ccode>t.Cleanup(fn)\u003c/code> registers cleanup. Use \u003ccode>httptest.NewServer\u003c/code> to start a fake HTTP server for client code, and \u003ccode>httptest.NewRecorder\u003c/code> to test handlers without a network. Combine with the fakes from the interfaces lesson. Keep tests deterministic: inject the clock and random sources instead of calling \u003ccode>time.Now\u003c/code> deep inside logic.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"coverage-and-benchmarks\">Coverage and benchmarks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"go test ./... -cover\ngo test ./... -coverprofile=cover.out\ngo tool cover -html=cover.out\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">go \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">test\u003c/span> ./... -cover\ngo \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">test\u003c/span> ./... -coverprofile=cover.out\ngo tool cover -html=cover.out\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Coverage shows what was executed, not whether it was checked well; use it to find untested branches, not as a target for its own sake. Benchmarks measure speed:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"func BenchmarkParseSize(b *testing.B) {\n    for i := 0; i &lt; b.N; i++ {\n        _, _ = ParseSize(&quot;512M&quot;)\n    }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title\">BenchmarkParseSize\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">(b *testing.B)\u003c/span>\u003c/span> {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> i := \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>; i &lt; b.N; i++ {\n        _, _ = ParseSize(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;512M&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"go test -bench=. -benchmem\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">go \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">test\u003c/span> -bench=. -benchmem\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Read \u003ccode>ns/op\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>allocs/op\u003c/code>, and compare before and after a change; the \u003ccode>benchstat\u003c/code> tool helps with noise. Measure first, then optimise.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tooling-in-ci\">Tooling in CI\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A reasonable pipeline for a Go service:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gofmt -l . | tee /dev/stderr | (! read)   # fail if anything is unformatted\ngo vet ./...\ngo test -race -cover ./...\ngo build ./...\ngovulncheck ./...                         # known vulnerabilities in dependencies and code\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">gofmt -l . | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tee\u003c/span> /dev/stderr | (! \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">read\u003c/span>)   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># fail if anything is unformatted\u003c/span>\ngo vet ./...\ngo \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">test\u003c/span> -race -cover ./...\ngo build ./...\ngovulncheck ./...                         \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># known vulnerabilities in dependencies and code\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Add a linter such as golangci-lint if the team wants more rules. Cache the module and build directories between runs to keep the pipeline fast. Pin the Go version in \u003ccode>go.mod\u003c/code> so developers and CI use the same toolchain.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A test passes locally and fails in CI only sometimes. What are two likely causes in Go code?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> A data race (run with -race) or a test that depends on time, ordering or shared state.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Make tests deterministic and use the race detector.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-100-percent-coverage-that-missed-the-bug\">Incident: The 100 percent coverage that missed the bug\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A report parser had full coverage and still sent wrong numbers to finance.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"go test ./report -cover\ngo test ./report -run TestMonth -v\n# month 12 is parsed as 0\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>go \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">test\u003c/span> ./report -cover\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ok  \texample.com/ops/report\t0.012s\tcoverage: 100.0% of statements\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>go \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">test\u003c/span> ./report -run TestMonth -v\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">--- PASS: TestMonth (0.00s)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># month 12 is parsed as 0\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Tests executed every line but only with a single month value that happened to work. The team replaced the example test with a table covering every month and boundary values, and added a rule that new parsing code needs edge cases (empty, minimum, maximum, invalid). They stopped using the coverage percentage as a goal and use it to find unexecuted branches.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Tests that depend on the network or the clock\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Chasing coverage numbers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not using subtests, so failures lack names\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping the race detector\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-first-test","text":"A first test"},{"id":"helpers-and-temporary-files","text":"Helpers and temporary files"},{"id":"coverage-and-benchmarks","text":"Coverage and benchmarks"},{"id":"tooling-in-ci","text":"Tooling in CI"},{"id":"incident-the-100-percent-coverage-that-missed-the-bug","text":"Incident: The 100 percent coverage that missed the bug"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Go testing package","u":"https://pkg.go.dev/testing"},{"t":"Add a test (Go tutorial)","u":"https://go.dev/doc/tutorial/add-a-test"}],"text":"Go has testing built in: a file ending in _test.go, a function starting with Test, and the go test command. There is no framework to choose or install. Combined with the race detector, benchmarks and vet, you get a solid safety net with very little setup, which is why Go projects are easy to contribute to and safe to change. What you will learn Write table-driven unit tests with subtests Use test helpers, temporary directories and fakes Measure coverage and write benchmarks Run vet, race and linters in CI A first test Tests live next to the code in _test.go files. // parse.go func ParseSize (s string ) ( int64 , error ) { /* ... */ } // parse_test.go func TestParseSize (t *testing.T) { tests := [] struct { name string in string want int64 wantErr bool }{ { \"bytes\" , \"512\" , 512 , false }, { \"kilobytes\" , \"2K\" , 2048 , false }, { \"empty\" , \"\" , 0 , true }, { \"junk\" , \"12Q\" , 0 , true }, } for _, tc := range tests { t.Run(tc.name, func (t *testing.T) { got, err := ParseSize(tc.in) if (err != nil ) != tc.wantErr { t.Fatalf( \"err = %v, wantErr %v\" , err, tc.wantErr) } if got != tc.want { t.Errorf( \"got %d, want %d\" , got, tc.want) } }) } } The table-driven style makes adding a case one line and gives each case a name in the output. Helpers and temporary files t.TempDir() gives a directory that is cleaned up after the test. t.Helper() marks a function as a helper so failures point at the caller. t.Cleanup(fn) registers cleanup. Use httptest.NewServer to start a fake HTTP server for client code, and httptest.NewRecorder to test handlers without a network. Combine with the fakes from the interfaces lesson. Keep tests deterministic: inject the clock and random sources instead of calling time.Now deep inside logic. Coverage and benchmarks go test ./... -cover go test ./... -coverprofile=cover.out go tool cover -html=cover.out Coverage shows what was executed, not whether it was checked well; use it to find untested branches, not as a target for its own sake. Benchmarks measure speed: func BenchmarkParseSize (b *testing.B) { for i := 0 ; i \u003c b.N; i++ { _, _ = ParseSize( \"512M\" ) } } go test -bench=. -benchmem Read ns/op and allocs/op , and compare before and after a change; the benchstat tool helps with noise. Measure first, then optimise. Tooling in CI A reasonable pipeline for a Go service: gofmt -l . | tee /dev/stderr | (! read ) # fail if anything is unformatted go vet ./... go test -race -cover ./... go build ./... govulncheck ./... # known vulnerabilities in dependencies and code Add a linter such as golangci-lint if the team wants more rules. Cache the module and build directories between runs to keep the pipeline fast. Pin the Go version in go.mod so developers and CI use the same toolchain. Predict first. A test passes locally and fails in CI only sometimes. What are two likely causes in Go code? Your guess Answer: A data race (run with -race) or a test that depends on time, ordering or shared state. Make tests deterministic and use the race detector. Incident: The 100 percent coverage that missed the bug Incident A report parser had full coverage and still sent wrong numbers to finance. $ go test ./report -cover ok example.com/ops/report 0.012s coverage: 100.0% of statements $ go test ./report -run TestMonth -v --- PASS: TestMonth (0.00s) $ # month 12 is parsed as 0 Tests executed every line but only with a single month value that happened to work. The team replaced the example test with a table covering every month and boundary values, and added a rule that new parsing code needs edge cases (empty, minimum, maximum, invalid). They stopped using the coverage percentage as a goal and use it to find unexecuted branches. Common mistakes Tests that depend on the network or the clock Chasing coverage numbers Not using subtests, so failures lack names Skipping the race detector"},"go/building-containers-and-cli-tools":{"html":"\u003cp>You wrote the tool, and now other people must run it: on laptops, in pipelines and in containers. A clear command-line interface, a version flag that tells you which build is running, and a small image with no shell for an attacker are the finishing touches that make a Go tool operable. Go makes each of these unusually easy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Parse flags and subcommands, and set exit codes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Embed the version at build time with ldflags\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Write a multi-stage Dockerfile for a static binary\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose a base image: scratch, distroless or alpine\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-command-line-tool\">A command-line tool\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The standard \u003ccode>flag\u003c/code> package is enough for simple tools:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"func main() {\n    addr := flag.String(&quot;addr&quot;, &quot;:8080&quot;, &quot;listen address&quot;)\n    verbose := flag.Bool(&quot;v&quot;, false, &quot;verbose logging&quot;)\n    showVersion := flag.Bool(&quot;version&quot;, false, &quot;print version and exit&quot;)\n    flag.Parse()\n\n    if *showVersion {\n        fmt.Println(version)\n        return\n    }\n    // run the tool with *addr and *verbose\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title\">main\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">()\u003c/span>\u003c/span> {\n    addr := flag.String(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;addr&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;:8080&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;listen address&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    verbose := flag.Bool(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;v&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;verbose logging&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    showVersion := flag.Bool(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;version&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;print version and exit&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    flag.Parse()\n\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> *showVersion {\n        fmt.Println(version)\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span>\n    }\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">// run the tool with *addr and *verbose\u003c/span>\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>For tools with many subcommands (\u003ccode>tool get\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>tool apply\u003c/code>), libraries such as cobra are common. Whatever you use: print usage on \u003ccode>-h\u003c/code>, write results to standard output and diagnostics to standard error, and exit non-zero on failure so pipelines can react. Read secrets from environment variables or files, not from flags, because flags show in the process list.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"embed-the-version\">Embed the version\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Put a variable in \u003ccode>main\u003c/code> and set it at build time:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"var version = &quot;dev&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">var\u003c/span> version = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dev&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"go build -ldflags &quot;-s -w -X main.version=$(git describe --tags --always)&quot; -o tool .\n./tool -version\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">go build -ldflags \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;-s -w -X main.version=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">$(git describe --tags --always)\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> -o tool .\n./tool -version\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>-X\u003c/code> sets the string, and \u003ccode>-s -w\u003c/code> strips debug data to make the binary smaller. Every binary and container should report exactly what it is, which makes incident questions like &quot;which build is running?&quot; easy to answer. Go also records module and VCS information in the binary, readable with \u003ccode>go version -m ./tool\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-multi-stage-image\">A multi-stage image\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Compile in a full Go image and copy only the binary into a minimal one:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"dockerfile\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"FROM golang:1.24 AS build\nWORKDIR /src\nCOPY go.mod go.sum ./\nRUN go mod download\nCOPY . .\nRUN CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=linux go build -trimpath -ldflags &quot;-s -w&quot; -o /out/tool .\n\nFROM gcr.io/distroless/static:nonroot\nCOPY --from=build /out/tool /tool\nUSER nonroot\nENTRYPOINT [&quot;/tool&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> golang:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1.24\u003c/span> AS build\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WORKDIR\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> /src\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> go.mod go.sum ./\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">RUN\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> go mod download\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> . .\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">RUN\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=linux go build -trimpath -ldflags \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;-s -w&quot;\u003c/span> -o /out/tool .\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> gcr.io/distroless/static:nonroot\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> --from=build /out/tool /tool\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">USER\u003c/span> nonroot\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ENTRYPOINT\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/tool&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Copying \u003ccode>go.mod\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>go.sum\u003c/code> first lets the dependency download layer be cached when only code changes. The final image holds one static binary and nothing else, often under 15 MB, with no shell, no package manager and few vulnerabilities to scan. The image runs as a non-root user.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing-the-base\">Choosing the base\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>scratch\u003c/code> is an empty image: smallest, but no CA certificates, time zone data or user accounts unless you copy them in. \u003cstrong>Distroless\u003c/strong> static images include certificates and a non-root user and nothing else, a good default. \u003cstrong>Alpine\u003c/strong> gives you a shell and package manager for debugging, at the cost of more surface and a different C library (not an issue for static Go builds). For debugging a distroless image in Kubernetes, use an ephemeral debug container instead of installing tools in production. Add a health check or rely on the orchestrator probes, and set resource limits, including \u003ccode>GOMEMLIMIT\u003c/code> so the Go runtime respects the container memory limit.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Your Go container needs HTTPS calls but the image is `scratch` and certificate errors appear. What are two fixes?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Copy the CA certificates into the image, or use a distroless static base which already includes them.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A bare image has no trust store.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-which-build-is-running\">Incident: Which build is running?\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A bug fix did not seem to work, and nobody could say whether production had the new binary.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl exec deploy/billing -- /billing -version\nkubectl get deploy billing -o jsonpath=&quot;{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> deploy/billing -- /billing -version\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">OCI runtime exec failed: exec: &quot;/billing&quot;: stat /billing: no such file or directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get deploy billing -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">registry.example.com/billing:latest\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The image used the latest tag and the binary had no version output, so nobody could confirm what ran. The team started tagging images with the commit hash, embedding the version with ldflags, exposing it at /version and in a metric, and recorded it in release notes. A debugging container showed the build info in seconds.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using latest tags\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running as root in the container\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting secrets in command-line flags\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting CA certificates in scratch images\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-command-line-tool","text":"A command-line tool"},{"id":"embed-the-version","text":"Embed the version"},{"id":"a-multi-stage-image","text":"A multi-stage image"},{"id":"choosing-the-base","text":"Choosing the base"},{"id":"incident-which-build-is-running","text":"Incident: Which build is running?"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Go command: build","u":"https://pkg.go.dev/cmd/go#hdr-Compile_packages_and_dependencies"},{"t":"flag package","u":"https://pkg.go.dev/flag"}],"text":"You wrote the tool, and now other people must run it: on laptops, in pipelines and in containers. A clear command-line interface, a version flag that tells you which build is running, and a small image with no shell for an attacker are the finishing touches that make a Go tool operable. Go makes each of these unusually easy. What you will learn Parse flags and subcommands, and set exit codes Embed the version at build time with ldflags Write a multi-stage Dockerfile for a static binary Choose a base image: scratch, distroless or alpine A command-line tool The standard flag package is enough for simple tools: func main () { addr := flag.String( \"addr\" , \":8080\" , \"listen address\" ) verbose := flag.Bool( \"v\" , false , \"verbose logging\" ) showVersion := flag.Bool( \"version\" , false , \"print version and exit\" ) flag.Parse() if *showVersion { fmt.Println(version) return } // run the tool with *addr and *verbose } For tools with many subcommands ( tool get , tool apply ), libraries such as cobra are common. Whatever you use: print usage on -h , write results to standard output and diagnostics to standard error, and exit non-zero on failure so pipelines can react. Read secrets from environment variables or files, not from flags, because flags show in the process list. Embed the version Put a variable in main and set it at build time: var version = \"dev\" go build -ldflags \"-s -w -X main.version= $(git describe --tags --always) \" -o tool . ./tool -version -X sets the string, and -s -w strips debug data to make the binary smaller. Every binary and container should report exactly what it is, which makes incident questions like \"which build is running?\" easy to answer. Go also records module and VCS information in the binary, readable with go version -m ./tool . A multi-stage image Compile in a full Go image and copy only the binary into a minimal one: FROM golang: 1.24 AS build WORKDIR /src COPY go.mod go.sum ./ RUN go mod download COPY . . RUN CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=linux go build -trimpath -ldflags \"-s -w\" -o /out/tool . FROM gcr.io/distroless/static:nonroot COPY --from=build /out/tool /tool USER nonroot ENTRYPOINT [ \"/tool\" ] Copying go.mod and go.sum first lets the dependency download layer be cached when only code changes. The final image holds one static binary and nothing else, often under 15 MB, with no shell, no package manager and few vulnerabilities to scan. The image runs as a non-root user. Choosing the base scratch is an empty image: smallest, but no CA certificates, time zone data or user accounts unless you copy them in. Distroless static images include certificates and a non-root user and nothing else, a good default. Alpine gives you a shell and package manager for debugging, at the cost of more surface and a different C library (not an issue for static Go builds). For debugging a distroless image in Kubernetes, use an ephemeral debug container instead of installing tools in production. Add a health check or rely on the orchestrator probes, and set resource limits, including GOMEMLIMIT so the Go runtime respects the container memory limit. Predict first. Your Go container needs HTTPS calls but the image is `scratch` and certificate errors appear. What are two fixes? Your guess Answer: Copy the CA certificates into the image, or use a distroless static base which already includes them. A bare image has no trust store. Incident: Which build is running? Incident A bug fix did not seem to work, and nobody could say whether production had the new binary. $ kubectl exec deploy/billing -- /billing -version OCI runtime exec failed: exec: \"/billing\": stat /billing: no such file or directory $ kubectl get deploy billing -o jsonpath= \"{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}\" registry.example.com/billing:latest The image used the latest tag and the binary had no version output, so nobody could confirm what ran. The team started tagging images with the commit hash, embedding the version with ldflags, exposing it at /version and in a m"},"go/debugging-go-programs":{"html":"\u003cp>A Go service in production fails in a few characteristic ways: a panic with a stack trace, memory that grows until the process is killed, CPU that spikes, or requests that hang. Go ships with excellent diagnostic tools, and most of them work on a running process. Knowing the handful of commands turns a vague report into a specific line of code.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Read a panic and a goroutine dump\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Find goroutine leaks and deadlocks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Profile CPU and memory with pprof\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use the race detector and delve for deeper investigation\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"read-the-trace\">Read the trace\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"panic: runtime error: invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference\n[signal SIGSEGV: segmentation violation code=0x1 addr=0x0 pc=0x4a1f3c]\n\ngoroutine 33 [running]:\nmain.(*Client).Fetch(0x0, {0xc000014080, 0x9})\n    /src/client.go:48 +0x1c\nmain.handle(...)\n    /src/server.go:91\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">panic: runtime error: invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference\n[signal SIGSEGV: segmentation violation code=0x1 addr=0x0 pc=0x4a1f3c]\n\ngoroutine 33 [running]:\nmain.(*Client).Fetch(0x0, {0xc000014080, 0x9})\n    /src/client.go:48 +0x1c\nmain.handle(...)\n    /src/server.go:91\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Read from the top: the first line says what happened, the first frame in your own code shows where (\u003ccode>client.go:48\u003c/code>), and the receiver value \u003ccode>0x0\u003c/code> shows the method was called on a nil pointer. Frames below show who called it. Most panics are nil pointers, out-of-range indexes, closed channels and nil map writes, each visible in that first line.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"leaks-and-hangs\">Leaks and hangs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If requests hang or memory grows, look at goroutines. Send \u003ccode>SIGQUIT\u003c/code> (Ctrl+backslash) to dump all stacks, or expose pprof:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"go\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import _ &quot;net/http/pprof&quot;\n\ngo func() { log.Println(http.ListenAndServe(&quot;127.0.0.1:6060&quot;, nil)) }()\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> _ \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;net/http/pprof&quot;\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">go\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">func\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">()\u003c/span>\u003c/span> { log.Println(http.ListenAndServe(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;127.0.0.1:6060&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">nil\u003c/span>)) }()\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s &quot;http://127.0.0.1:6060/debug/pprof/goroutine?debug=1&quot; | head -30\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">curl -s \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;http://127.0.0.1:6060/debug/pprof/goroutine?debug=1&quot;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -30\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Thousands of goroutines at the same line, for example \u003ccode>chan send\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>select\u003c/code>, is a leak: something is waiting for a receiver or a cancellation that never comes. \u003ccode>all goroutines are asleep - deadlock!\u003c/code> means every goroutine is blocked, usually on a channel or a lock. Bind the pprof port to localhost and reach it through a port-forward, never expose it publicly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"profiling\">Profiling\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"go tool pprof -http=:0 &quot;http://127.0.0.1:6060/debug/pprof/profile?seconds=30&quot;   # CPU\ngo tool pprof -http=:0 &quot;http://127.0.0.1:6060/debug/pprof/heap&quot;                # memory in use\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">go tool pprof -http=:0 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;http://127.0.0.1:6060/debug/pprof/profile?seconds=30&quot;\u003c/span>   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># CPU\u003c/span>\ngo tool pprof -http=:0 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;http://127.0.0.1:6060/debug/pprof/heap&quot;\u003c/span>                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># memory in use\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The flame graph and \u003ccode>top\u003c/code> view show which functions use the most CPU or hold the most memory. For memory, compare two heap profiles taken some minutes apart (\u003ccode>-base\u003c/code>) to see what grows. Also check \u003ccode>GODEBUG=gctrace=1\u003c/code> for garbage-collection pressure, and the \u003ccode>runtime/metrics\u003c/code> or Prometheus Go collector for goroutine count, heap size and GC pauses on dashboards. Profile before guessing: the slow part is rarely where you expect.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"races-and-the-debugger\">Races and the debugger\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The race detector (\u003ccode>go test -race\u003c/code>, or \u003ccode>go build -race\u003c/code> for a staging binary) reports two goroutines touching the same variable. Treat each report as a real bug. For logic errors, \u003cstrong>delve\u003c/strong> (\u003ccode>dlv debug\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>dlv test\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>dlv attach PID\u003c/code>) lets you set breakpoints and inspect variables, including goroutines. Add structured logging with request IDs, and in incidents capture a goroutine dump and a heap profile before restarting, because a restart destroys the evidence.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A service restarts every night because memory grows. What two profiles do you capture, and what do you compare?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Two heap profiles some time apart, and a goroutine dump. Compare them to see which allocations or goroutines grow.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Capture before restarting; compare to find growth.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-forty-thousand-goroutines\">Incident: Forty thousand goroutines\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Memory in a webhook forwarder climbed 100 MB per hour until the pod was killed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s &quot;http://127.0.0.1:6060/debug/pprof/goroutine?debug=1&quot; | head -4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;http://127.0.0.1:6060/debug/pprof/goroutine?debug=1&quot;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">goroutine profile: total 40871\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">40112 @ 0x43a2e6 0x4062b9 0x4057d5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">#\t0x4057d4\tmain.forward+0x94\t/src/forward.go:63\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each webhook started a goroutine that sent to an unbuffered results channel, but the receiver returned early on timeout, leaving the sender blocked forever. The fix used a buffered channel of size one and checked ctx.Done() in the sender. A goroutine-count alert was added to the dashboard so a repeat would be seen within an hour.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Restarting before capturing a dump\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Exposing pprof publicly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Guessing at performance without a profile\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring race detector reports\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"read-the-trace","text":"Read the trace"},{"id":"leaks-and-hangs","text":"Leaks and hangs"},{"id":"profiling","text":"Profiling"},{"id":"races-and-the-debugger","text":"Races and the debugger"},{"id":"incident-forty-thousand-goroutines","text":"Incident: Forty thousand goroutines"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Diagnostics","u":"https://go.dev/doc/diagnostics"},{"t":"Profiling Go Programs","u":"https://go.dev/blog/pprof"}],"text":"A Go service in production fails in a few characteristic ways: a panic with a stack trace, memory that grows until the process is killed, CPU that spikes, or requests that hang. Go ships with excellent diagnostic tools, and most of them work on a running process. Knowing the handful of commands turns a vague report into a specific line of code. What you will learn Read a panic and a goroutine dump Find goroutine leaks and deadlocks Profile CPU and memory with pprof Use the race detector and delve for deeper investigation Read the trace panic: runtime error: invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference [signal SIGSEGV: segmentation violation code=0x1 addr=0x0 pc=0x4a1f3c] goroutine 33 [running]: main.(*Client).Fetch(0x0, {0xc000014080, 0x9}) /src/client.go:48 +0x1c main.handle(...) /src/server.go:91 Read from the top: the first line says what happened, the first frame in your own code shows where ( client.go:48 ), and the receiver value 0x0 shows the method was called on a nil pointer. Frames below show who called it. Most panics are nil pointers, out-of-range indexes, closed channels and nil map writes, each visible in that first line. Leaks and hangs If requests hang or memory grows, look at goroutines. Send SIGQUIT (Ctrl+backslash) to dump all stacks, or expose pprof: import _ \"net/http/pprof\" go func () { log.Println(http.ListenAndServe( \"127.0.0.1:6060\" , nil )) }() curl -s \"http://127.0.0.1:6060/debug/pprof/goroutine?debug=1\" | head -30 Thousands of goroutines at the same line, for example chan send or select , is a leak: something is waiting for a receiver or a cancellation that never comes. all goroutines are asleep - deadlock! means every goroutine is blocked, usually on a channel or a lock. Bind the pprof port to localhost and reach it through a port-forward, never expose it publicly. Profiling go tool pprof -http=:0 \"http://127.0.0.1:6060/debug/pprof/profile?seconds=30\" # CPU go tool pprof -http=:0 \"http://127.0.0.1:6060/debug/pprof/heap\" # memory in use The flame graph and top view show which functions use the most CPU or hold the most memory. For memory, compare two heap profiles taken some minutes apart ( -base ) to see what grows. Also check GODEBUG=gctrace=1 for garbage-collection pressure, and the runtime/metrics or Prometheus Go collector for goroutine count, heap size and GC pauses on dashboards. Profile before guessing: the slow part is rarely where you expect. Races and the debugger The race detector ( go test -race , or go build -race for a staging binary) reports two goroutines touching the same variable. Treat each report as a real bug. For logic errors, delve ( dlv debug , dlv test , dlv attach PID ) lets you set breakpoints and inspect variables, including goroutines. Add structured logging with request IDs, and in incidents capture a goroutine dump and a heap profile before restarting, because a restart destroys the evidence. Predict first. A service restarts every night because memory grows. What two profiles do you capture, and what do you compare? Your guess Answer: Two heap profiles some time apart, and a goroutine dump. Compare them to see which allocations or goroutines grow. Capture before restarting; compare to find growth. Incident: Forty thousand goroutines Incident Memory in a webhook forwarder climbed 100 MB per hour until the pod was killed. $ curl -s \"http://127.0.0.1:6060/debug/pprof/goroutine?debug=1\" | head -4 goroutine profile: total 40871 40112 @ 0x43a2e6 0x4062b9 0x4057d5 # 0x4057d4 main.forward+0x94 /src/forward.go:63 Each webhook started a goroutine that sent to an unbuffered results channel, but the receiver returned early on timeout, leaving the sender blocked forever. The fix used a buffered channel of size one and checked ctx.Done() in the sender. A goroutine-count alert was added to the dashboard so a repeat would be seen within an hour. Common mistakes Restarting before capturing a dump Exposing pprof publicly Guessing at performance without a profile Ignoring race det"}});